    <div class="jr-answer">
      <h2>The short answer</h2>
      <p><strong>Most collagen trials used between 2.5g and 10g of hydrolysed collagen peptides a day, taken daily for 8 to 24 weeks; skin studies sit at the lower end and joint studies at the higher end.</strong> Undenatured type II collagen (UC-II) is a different ingredient dosed at just 40mg a day, about 250 times less, because it is thought to work through the immune system rather than as raw material.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Skin:</strong> trials used from 1g to 10g a day. A 64-woman trial found benefit at 1g of low-molecular-weight fish collagen; most used 2.5g to 10g.</li>
        <li><strong>Joints and bone:</strong> joint trials mostly used 5g to 12g a day; the main bone trial used 5g a day for 12 months in 131 postmenopausal women.</li>
        <li><strong>UC-II:</strong> 40mg a day in a 191-person, 180-day knee trial against placebo and glucosamine with chondroitin.</li>
        <li><strong>The honest limitation:</strong> no trial has shown that more works better, and no official upper limit exists. Consistency over 12 weeks matters more than the size of the scoop.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

