    <div class="jr-prose">

      <h2 id="by-goal">How much collagen should I take per day?</h2>

      <p>
        It depends on what you are taking it for, and the honest guide is the dose the relevant trials
        used. There is no official daily requirement for collagen, because your body makes its own and
        it is not an essential nutrient.
      </p>

      <div class="jr-table-scroll">
        <table class="jr-table">
          <thead><tr><th scope="col">Goal</th><th scope="col">Daily dose used in trials</th><th scope="col">Typical trial length</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><th scope="row">Skin hydration and elasticity</th><td>1g to 10g hydrolysed peptides, most often 2.5g to 10g</td><td>8 to 12 weeks</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Brittle nails</th><td>2.5g</td><td>24 weeks</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Joint pain and osteoarthritis</th><td>Mostly 5g to 12g hydrolysed peptides</td><td>12 weeks to 12 months</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Bone density after menopause</th><td>5g specific peptides</td><td>12 months</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Knee osteoarthritis (UC-II)</th><td>40mg undenatured type II</td><td>180 days</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p>
        <strong>Skin.</strong> A critical review of the skin meta-analyses found the trials they pooled
        used anywhere from 0.3g to 10g a day, which is one reason their results are hard to compare. At
        the low end, a 12-week trial in 64 women reported better hydration, elasticity and wrinkle depth
        on 1g a day of low-molecular-weight fish collagen against placebo. Most skin trials sit between
        2.5g and 10g. Our article on <a href="/pages/collagen-for-skin">collagen for skin</a> covers why
        the strength of that evidence is debated.
      </p>

      <p>
        <strong>Joints.</strong> Joint trials generally used more. Recent examples include 5g a day for
        12 weeks in 180 active young adults with knee discomfort, 10g a day for 8 weeks in 160 adults
        with osteoarthritis, and an ongoing trial of 12g a day for 12 months in 302 postmenopausal women
        with knee osteoarthritis. One Korean trial used 3g of low-molecular-weight peptides for six
        months, though a published commentary questioned its design.
      </p>

      <p>
        <strong>Bone.</strong> The best-known bone trial gave 131 postmenopausal women 5g a day of
        specific collagen peptides or placebo for 12 months, and density improved at the spine and
        femoral neck in the collagen group. We cover it in
        <a href="/pages/how-to-increase-bone-density">how to increase bone density</a>.
      </p>

