      <h2 id="uc-ii">Why is UC-II only 40mg?</h2>

      <p>
        Because it is a different ingredient that is thought to work in a different way. Hydrolysed
        collagen is broken into small peptides that are absorbed and act partly as building blocks and
        signals. Undenatured type II collagen, sold as UC-II and usually made from chicken sternum
        cartilage, keeps its original structure and is thought to act on the immune tissue of the gut,
        a process called oral tolerance. That needs only a tiny amount.
      </p>

      <p>
        The key trial, published in <em>Nutrition Journal</em> in 2016, gave 191 people with knee
        osteoarthritis 40mg of UC-II, 1,500mg of glucosamine with 1,200mg of chondroitin, or placebo
        for 180 days, and reported better knee scores on UC-II than on either. The ingredient was
        supplied by its manufacturer, and the authors themselves said more research was needed to
        confirm the result.
      </p>

      <p>
        The practical point is the 250-fold difference. A 40mg UC-II capsule is not a weaker version of a
        10g collagen shot, and a 10g shot is not an overdose of UC-II. They are different products. What
        should worry you is the third kind of label: a hydrolysed collagen sold at 40mg or 50mg, borrowing
        UC-II's dose without UC-II's mechanism. That is too little hydrolysed collagen to match any trial.
      </p>

