      <h2 id="too-much">Is 10g of collagen too much?</h2>

      <p>
        No. 10g sits inside the range used in joint trials, and there is no officially established upper
        limit for collagen peptides. But more is not better: no trial has shown a clear dose-response,
        where 15g works better than 10g, or 10g better than 5g, for the same goal.
      </p>

      <p>
        There are two sensible reasons not to go far beyond the trial range. First, long-term safety data
        are thin: few trials ran beyond about 24 weeks. Second, collagen is rich in hydroxyproline, which
        the body partly turns into oxalate, relevant if you have had calcium oxalate kidney stones. We did
        the arithmetic on our own labels in <a href="/pages/collagen-side-effects">collagen side
        effects</a>: about 920mg of hydroxyproline in Skelecoll's 10,000mg and about 740mg in
        Collabloom's 8,000mg, under the 1,000mg level that did not change urinary oxalate in a small
        trial.
      </p>

      <p>
        Skelecoll's 10,000mg sits at the upper end of the joint trials, and Collabloom's 8,000mg within
        the skin range. Neither is a reason to take two shots a day. If you want to judge a collagen
        supplement fairly, take one full dose every day for 12 weeks; our guide to
        <a href="/pages/how-long-does-collagen-take-to-work">how long collagen takes to work</a> explains
        why stopping early makes the test meaningless.
      </p>

      <h2 id="labels">How to check the dose on a label</h2>

      <ol>
        <li><strong>Find the collagen line in milligrams per serving.</strong> If it is hidden inside a proprietary blend, you cannot know whether you are getting a trial dose.</li>
        <li><strong>Check the type.</strong> Hydrolysed collagen or collagen peptides should be in grams, roughly 2.5g to 10g. Undenatured type II should be around 40mg.</li>
        <li><strong>Check servings per day.</strong> A pack that needs six capsules to reach 2.5g is a different product from one that delivers it in a single serving.</li>
        <li><strong>Look at the price per gram,</strong> not per pack. A cheaper pack at 50mg per capsule can be the most expensive collagen per gram on the shelf.</li>
      </ol>

      <h2 id="protein">Does collagen count towards daily protein?</h2>

      <p>
        Partly. Collagen is protein, so 10g of collagen adds roughly 10g to your intake, which matters in
        Indian diets that are often low in protein. But collagen is an incomplete protein: it contains no
        tryptophan and little leucine, so it should not replace dal, paneer, curd, eggs, fish or meat as
        your main protein source. Think of it as a targeted supplement, not as your protein for the day.
      </p>

