      <h2 id="faq">Frequently asked questions</h2>
      <div class="jr-faq">
        <details>
          <summary>How much collagen should I take per day?</summary>
          <p>Most trials used 2.5g to 10g of hydrolysed collagen peptides daily: skin studies at the lower end, joint studies at the higher end. Undenatured type II collagen (UC-II) is a separate ingredient dosed at about 40mg a day.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>How much collagen per day for joints?</summary>
          <p>Joint trials mostly used 5g to 12g of hydrolysed collagen a day for 12 weeks to 12 months. UC-II trials used 40mg a day. Take it daily and judge the effect after about 12 weeks.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>How much collagen per day for skin?</summary>
          <p>Skin trials used from 1g to 10g a day, most often 2.5g to 10g, for 8 to 12 weeks. A 64-woman trial found improvements at 1g of low-molecular-weight fish collagen, but most evidence comes from higher doses.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Is 10g of collagen a day too much?</summary>
          <p>No. 10g is within the range used in joint trials and no official upper limit exists. There is also no evidence that going higher works better, and long-term safety beyond about 24 weeks is less well studied.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Why is UC-II 40mg when collagen peptides are 10g?</summary>
          <p>They are different ingredients. UC-II keeps collagen's original structure and is thought to act through the immune tissue of the gut, which needs only a tiny amount. Hydrolysed peptides are dosed in grams.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Can I take collagen twice a day?</summary>
          <p>You can, but there is no evidence that splitting or doubling the dose improves results. One full daily dose, taken consistently, matches how the trials were run.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Does collagen count as protein?</summary>
          <p>Yes, roughly gram for gram, but it is incomplete. Collagen lacks tryptophan and is low in leucine, so it should not replace complete protein sources such as dal, paneer, curd, eggs or fish.</p>
        </details>
      </div>

